Ram disk memory type for loader.efi memdisk=: EfiLoaderData ->
EfiReservedMemoryType, so the kernel does not reclaim a ram disk root.
ACPI requires Reserved for a buffer registered with EFI_RAM_DISK_PROTOCOL.
The md fallback uses it too: the OS loader type UEFI allows instead
(0x80000000) hangs GetMemoryMap() on a ThinkPad T420 (tested 2026-11-08).
Needs md_preload_mapdev.patch in the booted kernel when the ram disk can
land above the direct map.

Bug report: FreeBSD/docs/bugreport_loader_memdisk_memtype.md
Investigation: FreeBSD/iPXE.md, section 7(f) "The fallback needed four fixes"

diff --git a/stand/efi/loader/decompress.c b/stand/efi/loader/decompress.c
index fbf800f..8288b71 100644
--- a/stand/efi/loader/decompress.c
+++ b/stand/efi/loader/decompress.c
@@ -20,13 +20,12 @@
 #define ULL(x) ((unsigned long long)(x))
 
 /*
- * While memdisk_uefi.efi used EfiReservedMemoryType, we need to use
- * EfiLoaderData instead, the standard memory type the loader uses to pass
- * memory to the kernel. This is also safe if we're chain booting to another efi
- * progarm since the memory will be available to that program. The other type
- * isn't necessarily in the pmap, so the md driver fails.
+ * The ram disk is the root filesystem for as long as the kernel runs, so it
+ * must not be EfiLoaderData: the kernel reuses those pages.  The kernel skips
+ * EfiReservedMemoryType.  ACPI requires it for a registered RAM disk, and the
+ * OS loader types UEFI suggests instead hang some firmware in GetMemoryMap().
  */
-static EFI_MEMORY_TYPE mem_type = EfiLoaderData;
+static EFI_MEMORY_TYPE mem_type = EfiReservedMemoryType;
 
 struct decomp_state
 {
